## Slow streets

The Jordaan was thrown up in the seventeenth century for workers and immigrants and is now one of the quietest and most expensive corners of the centre. The pleasure of it is that there is nothing in particular you are meant to see. You walk, and the streets do the work.

Start at the Westerkerk, follow the Prinsengracht north, and turn off wherever a side street looks promising. Watch for the hofjes, small almshouse courtyards behind plain doors, several of which still let visitors in during the day.

Saturday brings the Noordermarkt: produce in the morning, and a flea market that has been going for longer than anyone can remember.

